posts.body:
Hi. I'm trying to change the name of events spawned by Galv's event spawner using the following code (where $gameVariables.value(50) contains what I want to set the name to - which is a number that goes up per event spawned so the first event is 0, 2nd is 1, 3rd is 2, etc -- so each event should be named the number it was generated). Whenever I do this, then try to access the name through the code below to test it in a script attached to each event based on the template event - EVERY event EVER spawned only has the latest event name (so if I spawned 4 events - EVERY event will be named (3)) -- How do I fix this?! It's driving me mad.
JavaScript:
var lastEventId = $gameMap.events().length;
var newEvent = $gameMap.event(lastEventId);

if (newEvent) {
    newEvent.event().name = $gameVariables.value(50);
    alert($gameVariables.value(50));
    $gameMap.requestRefresh();
}
JavaScript:
alert(this.character(0).event().name);
alert($gameMap.event(this._eventId).event().name);

As I said, writing to the data level is more critical and often leads to unexpected situations. Furthermore, when the player goes to title screen, loads another save, etc. it will break with a 99% change.
For example, you can temporarily change an event's graphic to something else. The game logic will put this information into the game level, not the data level. You can put information to an event, like this:
Code:
$gameMap.event(1).myProperty = "asdf"
However, as we all know, all events are reset when changing the map, and so are your overrides. So I doubt that this is what you need.
A common way to store information is to bind it to $gameSystem. Information in $gameSystem is not removed on map changes, and the engine will also manage it when the player reloads or starts a new game. It is by far the easiest method.
Code:
$gameSystem.myProperty = "asdf"


// anywhere else in the game:
alert ("This is my property: " + $gameSystem.myProperty)
As your collection of information is more complex, you need to plan how to structure it. I bet it's structured like this? Map ID -> Event ID
In this case, you should maps instead of arrays.
Code:
// initialize your container, or skip if already done
$gameSystem.customEventData = $gameSystem.customEventData || { };

// write info
const key = "" + $gameMap.mapId() + "_" + eventId // <-- replace eventId
$gameSystem.customEventData[key] = "asdf"

// read info
const key = "" + $gameMap.mapId() + "_" + eventId // <-- replace eventId
const data = $gameSystem.customEventData[key];
alert(data);
